{{also|Hunky}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhʌŋki/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-hunky.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|en|hunk|y}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} Exhibiting strong, masculine beauty.
# Shaped like a [[hunk]], or piece; [[chunky]].
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} All right; in good condition.
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} [[even]]; [[square]]; on equal footing ''with''
#* '''1900''', {{w|Stephen Crane}}, ''Wounds in the Rain''
#*: {{...}} he dropped like a brick into the firing line and began to shoot; began to get "'''hunky'''" with all those people who had been plugging at him.

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|hunk}}

===Etymology 2===
From the older {{m|en||*hunk}}, probably alteration of {{m|en|Hungarian}}. Compare {{m|en|bohunk}} and {{l|en|honky}} / {{l|en|honkey}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|hunkie}}
* {{l|en|hunkey}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|slang|now|uncommon|ethnic slur}} A [[Hungarian]] or other eastern European, e.g. a [[Romanian]] or a [[Slav]]. {{q|Sometimes applied (like {{m|en|honky}}) to any [[white]] person.}}
#* '''1924''', Jack Bethea, ''Bed Rock'', page 175:
#*: "All '''hunkies''' and wops, and no wonder there was seven hundred and fifty of them."
#* '''1940''', ''Unemployment Compensation Interpretation Service: Benefit series'', page 183:
#*: He made hunkies and cut ice-cream sandwiches.
#* '''1952''', Chester Himes, ''Cast the First Stone'', page 66: 
#*: The night before I had let a '''hunky''' called Big John have a dollar's worth of chips in the poker game {{...}}
#* '''1969''', Robert Beck (Iceberg Slim), ''Trick Baby'', page 149:
#*: He said, "I'm going to buy this building and turn this into a Nigger bar. I'm going to bar all you fucking '''hunkies'''."
#* '''1994''', Josephine Wtulich, ''American Xenophobia and the Slav Immigrant: A Living Legacy of Mind and Spirit'':
#*: On the negative side, a '''hunky''' was culturally schizophrenic, an inhabitant of crowded and ill-kept rooms and whose clothing was in poor taste, an alcoholic, intrinsically dull and stupid, an offspring of domineering parents, {{...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=Victor Bockris|title=Warhol: The Biography|page=20|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=NRmjRcSYXlgC&lpg=PA20&dq=hunkies&hl=ru&pg=PA20#v=onepage&q=hunkies&f=false|passage=Like blacks, who were the only ethnic group below them on the social scale, Eastern Europeans, contemptuously labelled ‘'''hunkies'''’, were dismissed as incapable and untrustworthy.}}

